<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
	<!--
	  Universal Links for invites. Claims https://libstack.app/i/<token> so a tapped
	  link opens the app instead of Safari.

	  Requires /.well-known/apple-app-site-association to be served at that host over
	  HTTPS, with Content-Type application/json, no file extension, and NO redirect.
	  The OS enforces all three and fails silently when any is wrong -- the link just
	  opens the browser and nothing says why.

	  The AASA must scope itself to /i/* rather than /*, or the app also claims the
	  marketing pages and the /@handle profiles that are meant to be web pages.

	  See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-30-invite-landing-design.md.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:libstack.app</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The container the home-screen widget reads its snapshot out of.

	  The widget extension cannot reach Supabase: `reading_days` is owner-only RLS and an
	  extension has no auth session, and a timeline provider doing network I/O is the standard
	  way a widget goes blank. So the app writes a snapshot here -- the run, the last recorded
	  day, the book in progress, and the two hour constants -- and the extension only reads it.
	  The same group must be named in ios/StreakWidget/StreakWidget.entitlements and in
	  AppDelegate's `bookworm/widget` channel, or the write lands somewhere the widget cannot
	  see while appearing to succeed.

	  **This breaks device and TestFlight builds until the App ID carries the APP_GROUPS
	  capability**, which cloud managed signing cannot add for itself -- it can reissue a profile
	  for capabilities an App ID already has, and App Groups are not in the App Store Connect
	  API's surface at all. Register `group.com.unicorn.bookwormFriends` under Identifiers >
	  App Groups, tick App Groups on the App ID, and then `./release_ios.sh` works again.
	  `.venv/bin/python scripts/asc_key_probe.py` reports whether that has been done.

	  The simulator does not validate entitlements against a provisioning profile, which is why
	  the widget is fully testable locally before any of that.

	  See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-22-streak-widget-design.md.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.unicorn.bookwormFriends</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
